        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00003",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00296:title:sect-00003-title",
        "text": "Diazonium Salts: The Sandmeyer Reaction"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00009",
          "segmentId": "m00296:para:para-00009"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00005",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00296:alt:fig-00005-alt",
              "text": "Aniline reacts with nitrous acid and sulfuric acid to form arenediazonium salt, hydrogen sulfate, and two equivalents of water."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_08_006.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00010",
          "segmentId": "m00296:para:para-00010"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00006",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00296:alt:fig-00006-alt",
              "text": "The arenediazonium salt reacts with H S O 4 minus and nucleophile to form a benzene substituted with a nucleophile and nitrogen as the product."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_08_007.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00011",
          "segmentId": "m00296:para:para-00011"
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00012",
          "segmentId": "m00296:para:para-00012"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00007",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00296:alt:fig-00007-alt",
              "text": "p-Methylaniline and aniline both undergo reactions with nitrous acid and sulfuric acid to form two different intermediates. The first intermediate forms p-bromotoluene and the second one forms iodobenzene."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_08_008.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00013",
          "segmentId": "m00296:para:para-00013"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00008",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00296:alt:fig-00008-alt",
              "text": "o-Methylaniline reacts with nitrous acid and sulfuric acid to form o-methylbenzene-diazonium bisulfate. This reacts with potassium cyanide and copper cyanide to form o-methylbenzonitrile. This reacts with hydronium forming o-methylbenzoic acid."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_08_009.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00014",
          "segmentId": "m00296:para:para-00014"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00009",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00296:alt:fig-00009-alt",
              "text": "p-Methylaniline reacts with nitrous acid and sulfuric acid to form an intermediate. This reacts with copper oxide, copper nitrate, and water to form p-cresol with a 93 percent yield."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_08_010.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00015",
          "segmentId": "m00296:para:para-00015"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00010",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00296:alt:fig-00010-alt",
              "text": "p-Methylaniline reacts with two equivalents of bromine, then nitrous and sulfuric acids, then phosphinic acid to form 3,5-dibromotoluene. Toluene reacts with two equivalents of bromine and ferric bromide forming 2,4-dibromotoluene."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_08_011.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00016",
          "segmentId": "m00296:para:para-00016"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00011",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00296:alt:fig-00011-alt",
              "text": "Diazonium compound reacts with copper halide to form an aryl radical. This reacts to form a product in which benzene is substituted with an X group."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_08_012.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "id": "exam-00001",
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00296:example-title:exam-00001-title",
            "text": "Using Diazonium Replacement Reactions"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00017",
              "segmentId": "m00296:para:para-00017"
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00018",
              "segmentId": "m00296:para:para-00018",
              "title": {
                "segmentId": "m00296:para-title:para-00018-title",
                "text": "Strategy"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00019",
              "segmentId": "m00296:para:para-00019"
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00020",
              "title": {
                "segmentId": "m00296:para-title:para-00020-title",
                "text": "Solution"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00012",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00296:alt:fig-00012-alt",
              "text": "The structure of m-hydroxyacetophenone. It comprises a benzene ring with an acetyl group on C 1 and a hydroxyl group on C 3 position."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_08_013.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00013",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00296:alt:fig-00013-alt",
              "text": "Benzene reacts with acetyl chloride and aluminum chloride to form acetophenone. This reacts with four different sets of reagents in four different steps to form m-hydroxyacetophenone."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_08_014.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
